# Main Rules
- DO NOT EVER run build commands (yarn, npm, etc.) yourself UNLESS CLEARLY EXPLICITLY INSTRUCTED TO. This is because the commands are normally run in a sandbox by user.
- Project is typically built in Sandboxie sandbox (`JoplinSourceDev`), so file references like "logs can be found here: D:\TEMP\xfs-63dd4047\build.log" are most likely here: e:\Sandboxie\Solf\JoplinSourceDev\drive\D\TEMP\xfs-63dd4047\build.log
- Sandboxie note: real-filesystem paths (e.g. `e:\jps\`) must use "Open for All" access in sandbox config. If builds fail (e.g. "The current directory is invalid."), first check whether files are being created inside the sandbox layer (`e:\Sandboxie\Solf\JoplinSourceDev\drive\...`) instead of on the real filesystem.
- Generate comprehensible code, not convoluted
- Do not rewrite code that doesn't need rewriting
- **Three-letter TODO tags** (`qqq`, `zzz`, `fff`, `ddd`, `aaa`, etc.) are task markers. You may act on them when relevant. When you do: (1) add a `// rrr` comment **above** the original tag line explaining what you did and why; (2) keep the original tag text intact. Both `rrr` and the original tag are removed by the author during review.
- Do not convert leading tabs to spaces or the other way around (preserve existing)
- **Line endings**: new files use **LF** (unix). Existing files retain their current line endings to avoid massive diffs.
- Specs files and rule files are supposed to be terse and information-dense; important data should not be obscured by verbose syntax/formatting.

# Code Quality

## Fragile Decisions
- **Flag fragile decisions at two levels**:
  - *Design level* (architecture, approach): stop and ask the user **before writing code**. Prefer the robust solution unless the user explicitly opts for the shortcut.
  - *Implementation level* (code-level choices not covered by prior discussion): write the code but add `// rrr <explanation>` at the dubious spot so it surfaces during review. Examples: implicit ordering assumptions, edge cases handled with a heuristic, fallback values chosen without spec backing.

# Code Change Guidelines
- Minimize diff from upstream for easier re-patching on version upgrades. Do not refactor original code structure when adding functionality.

# Dev Workflow
- `yarn start` does NOT compile TypeScript -- it only bundles existing `.js` via esbuild.
- To see `.ts` changes: `yarn workspace @joplin/lib run tsc && yarn start` (from `packages/app-desktop`).
- For continuous dev: `yarn watch` in a separate terminal recompiles on change.

# Build Architecture
- Two bundles produced by esbuild (`packages/app-desktop/tools/bundleJs.ts`):
  - `main.bundle.js` -- Electron main process
  - `main-html.bundle.js` -- renderer/UI (search, note list, etc.)
- esbuild bundles `.js` not `.ts` (`prefer-js-imports` plugin). `tsc` must run first.

# Patching Real Joplin Install
- Patch both bundles into `app.asar` via 7z (run from `packages\app-desktop\`):
  `set ASAR_TARGET=e:\j_p\current\APP\resources\app.asar`
  `pushd e:\jps\joplin\packages\app-desktop\`
  `7z.exe a -tasar -i!main.bundle.js -ad -slp- -- "%ASAR_TARGET%"`
  `7z.exe a -tasar -i!main-html.bundle.js -ad -slp- -- "%ASAR_TARGET%"`
  WARNING: `-i!` path matches both outside and inside archive, must run from dir where paths match.
- Dev tools in real install: Help > Toggle Development Tools.

# Settings & Search Architecture
- Setting definitions live in `packages/lib/models/settings/builtInMetadata.ts` (not `Setting.ts` itself). `Setting.ts` only has migration/wasGlobal metadata.
- Settings with `public: true` appear automatically in Joplin's settings UI panel under their `section` (e.g. `'note'`).
- Middleware in `BaseApplication.ts:504` auto-refreshes note list when any setting key starting with `notes.sortOrder` changes. It does NOT watch `search.sortOrder` -- search results re-fetch on every new search, so a custom `search.*` setting takes effect on next search without middleware changes. To force immediate refresh when setting changes mid-search, add a check in that same middleware block.
- `SearchEngineUtils.notesForQuery()` is the single callsite for desktop search results (`BaseApplication.ts:256`). It calls `SearchEngine.search()` for FTS results, then `Note.previews()` to get fresh note data. Our custom sort is appended after the original relevance-order loop.
- `Note.previewFields()` already includes `user_updated_time`, `user_created_time`, `title`, `order`, `todo_due`, `todo_completed` -- so sorting by any of these requires no extra DB fields.
- `Note.fieldToLabel()` maps field names to UI labels (`title`, `user_updated_time`, etc.). PR #7622 added `bm25` for relevance but upstream didn't merge it; we use `'relevance'` as a synthetic option key instead.

# Known Bugs (upstream)
- OCR overwrites `resource.user_updated_time` via `autoTimestamp` in `Resource.save()` (`OcrService.ts:175`). Also `SearchEngine.syncTables_()` stores `resource.updated_time` (system time) into `items_normalized.user_updated_time` instead of `resource.user_updated_time`.

